            <ArticleTitle>Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in the dog: breed associations, nutritional considerations, and long-term outcome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>104-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1053/j.tcam.2012.04.004</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1938-9736(12)00006-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Canine exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (EPI) is an alimentary tract disorder causing malabsorption and debilitations in affected individuals. This article covers predisposing factors to EPI and response to therapy. Although relatively easy to diagnose, knowledge of breed predispositions (and also of those breeds where the disease is less common) can guide the clinician. Numerous studies have examined therapy for EPI, and a key finding is the variability in response among affected dogs. This implies that close monitoring and individual tailoring of therapy is needed to maximize the chance of success. Important factors affecting outcome are the choice of enzyme preparation, presence of hypocobalaminemia, and the response to the first 2 to 3 months of therapy.</AbstractText>
